                          "content": "started while nadim was in drift — “irrelevant to this goal”, Sobhi, 2026-08-15",
                          "lineHeight": 1.4,
                          "fontFamily": "$font-sans",
                          "fontSize": 11,
                          "fontWeight": "normal"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "id": "w1ky",
                      "name": "n",
                      "fill": "$muted",
                      "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                      "width": "fill_container",
                      "content": "The acknowledgement travels with the work. If the goal later goes wrong, the premise it was started on is on the record and not reconstructed from memory.",
                      "lineHeight": 1.5,
                      "fontFamily": "$font-sans",
                      "fontSize": 11,
                      "fontWeight": "normal"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "frame",
                  "id": "w1kz",
                  "name": "not",
                  "width": "fill_container",
                  "fill": "$surface",
                  "cornerRadius": "$radius",
                  "stroke": "$line",
                  "strokeWidth": 1,
                  "layout": "vertical",
                  "gap": 10,
                  "padding": 14,
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "id": "w1l0",
                      "name": "lbl",
                      "fill": "$muted",
                      "content": "WHAT THIS IS NOT",
                      "fontFamily": "$font-mono",
                      "fontSize": 10,
                      "fontWeight": "700",
                      "letterSpacing": 1
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "id": "w1l1",
                      "name": "t",
                      "fill": "$muted",
                      "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                      "width": "fill_container",
                      "content": "Not a block — a system that refuses to work because a document is stale gets routed around within a week. Not an interstitial either: it is folded into a gate the goal already had to pass, so it costs no extra click and cannot become a dialog people learn to dismiss.",
                      "lineHeight": 1.5,
                      "fontFamily": "$font-sans",
                      "fontSize": 11,
                      "fontWeight": "normal"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "w1l2",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -560,
      "name": "Round 3 — title",
      "width": 2980,
      "height": 480,
      "content": "AGENTIC OS — ROUND 3: THE EXECUTION LAYER AND THE APP SHELL (wireframe)\nBrief: docs/DESIGN-BRIEF-EXECUTION.md · pairs with PRD §12 and D18–D22 · inherits the round-1/2 register, tokens and status palette unchanged.\n\nTHE COMMISSION was the shell, not the screens. Two layers, two tempos, one window: a portfolio consulted for 3–10 seconds and closed, and an execution layer you sit inside for hours while it changes on its own. Those want opposite designs.\n\nRECOMMENDATION: ship C — the portfolio as a persistent spine, the project as the pane (frames C1–C5). B′ (frames B′1–B′2) is the honest challenger and is shown with the fix the brief asked for — a persistent drift ribbon that survives the mode switch. The argument for C is in the rationale note below C1/C2; the argument against it is in the note under B′.\n\nDATA HONESTY (brief §8 — the trap that produced two entries in docs/LEARNINGS.md):\n· Portfolio values are the snapshot measured 2026-07-26 (portfolio brief §4) and are rendered with that moment as \"now\", including the 12-day age of nadim's drift.\n· The drift string is the real one, verbatim: nadim/docs/HANDOFF.md claims \"PRs #1 through #22 merged\"; #23 is merged and #24 has been open since 2026-07-14.\n· Real task and goal strings throughout: \"Exercise the live reflection round-trip — Unblocked by: Sobhi pasting a real key into .env.local\" (nadim BACKLOG), \"Correct docs/HANDOFF.md PR frontier claim\", the nine-step compound-engineer chain and the agent roster (AgentOS-spec §4, §10), the real branch feat/desk-4a-prototype.\n· INVENTED, because no session has ever run: every cost, duration, tool call, session id, commit sha, inbox question and progress-log line. They are plausible rather than flattering — sessions take hours, costs are in tens of dollars, one session failed and produced no commit, and one runner returned no cost at all so it is excluded from the derived figures rather than counted as zero."
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "w1l3",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 940,
      "name": "Rationale — Concept C (recommended)",
      "width": 2980,
      "height": 200,
      "content": "CONCEPT C — PORTFOLIO AS A PERSISTENT SPINE, PROJECT AS THE PANE   ·   frames C1, C2, C3, C4, C5\nThe claim being tested: the portfolio is not a navigation index, it is a live instrument. In a two-tier sidebar it is a menu item; in a mode switch it is a place you left; here it is peripheral vision — which is the \"ambient glance\" moment the existing brief identified and could never serve while the Board was a destination.\n+ Optimises: the six rows never disappear, so the glance is satisfied permanently rather than on visit; one action to any project and one action back (⌘1 works inside a running session); horizontal tabs physically cap at five, which ENFORCES the work/setup split instead of encouraging it — Agents, Templates, Environments and Repos cannot creep into the navigation because there is nowhere to put them.\n− Sacrifices: 268px of horizontal room, forever, including while you are deep in a Kanban; the rail does two jobs at once, and if the roster ever goes loud it will be loud while you are working. Mitigated by giving the spine the lowest-resolution version of the signal it is allowed to carry: glyph + name, no counts, no sentences, no deltas — the sentence lives on the Board and nowhere else, so the two can never disagree."
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "w1l4",
      "x": 4620,
      "y": 940,
      "name": "Rationale — Concept B′ (the challenger)",
      "width": 2980,
      "height": 200,
      "content": "CONCEPT B′ — MODE SWITCH, WITH A PERSISTENT DRIFT RIBBON   ·   frames B′1, B′2\nThe brief said the thing that would beat C is a version of B that solves losing sight of the portfolio. This is that version: a 40px ribbon across the top of every project screen carrying all six glyphs and names, the shared freshness timestamp, and ← All projects.\n+ Optimises: a genuinely undisturbed interior — nothing about the other five projects competes while you work; the project rail can grow without ever costing the portfolio; the full window width belongs to the Kanban.\n− Sacrifices: the ribbon is a strictly worse instrument than the spine, because horizontal space forces the six names to compete with the ribbon's own controls, and severity has to survive at 11px in a line you read left-to-right rather than scan down; switching projects still costs two actions; and the swap on entry is a real disorientation that C simply does not have.\nWHY C STILL WINS: B′ pays for the interior by making the portfolio a strip you can stop seeing, and this product's entire premise is that you never have to remember to look. But B′ is the right answer if the Kanban proves cramped in build — the ribbon is the fallback, and it is designed here so that decision does not need a new round."
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "w1l5",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 2880,
      "name": "Positions on the ten open questions",
      "width": 2980,
      "height": 310,
      "content": "THE BRIEF SAID TAKE A POSITION, NOT ASK BACK. Ten positions, each visible in a frame.\n1 · ARCHITECTURE: C. Transition in frame C4 — the pane swaps, the spine never does, so there is no transition to design and that is the point.\n2 · DOES THE SWITCHER SHOW DRIFT? Yes, at the lowest resolution the signal has: glyph + name only. It is not a second source — it is the same collector and the same timestamp rendered smaller, and the spine carries no sentence, count or delta that could contradict the Board. The freshness line at the spine's foot is the Board's own, so they cannot drift apart.\n3 · EXECUTED VS HAND-MOVED: two different typographic registers, inherited from the Board's claim-versus-fact split one layer down. Executed = ▶ + monospace + a session id and a sha. Hand-moved = quoted italic sans naming a person. You can tell them apart across the room, before reading either.\n4 · RUNNING: a 3px $muted rail on the card's left edge plus a counter in monospace. No colour, no motion, no glow, no fourth status hue. It is an instrument reading, like the Board's freshness dot — and the running item is the one you are allowed to ignore, so it gets the quietest treatment in the product.\n5 · WHERE THE INBOX LIVES: a project surface, with its open count summed once in the spine. Not a contradiction of anti-goal 4 — that rule forbids duplicating the portfolio's question inside a project; this is the reverse, and it is a pointer rather than a surface. The count is the only badge in the product and its goal state is zero.\n6 · IS ▶ A FOURTH GLYPH? No — a different system, deliberately. Health (⚠ ○ ✓ ◌) states the world, leads a row, and always carries a status colour. Provenance (▶ ◆ ✓ ~) states an origin, lives in the evidence line, and is never coloured. Frame C5 shows them side by side; the shared ✓ is the one collision, resolved by colour and by position.\n7 · THE PRE-FLIGHT: folded into the DoD approval the goal already has to pass (frame P1). Not an interstitial — an extra dialog is a dialog people learn to dismiss, and this one must survive being seen fifty times.\n8 · A RAIL NEAR ITS CAP: type size, $attention, and a stated consequence in words (\"$2.88 left — about one more session\"; \"the session running now commits first\"). No progress bar: a bar implies a rate this product cannot verify.\n9 · A PROJECT WITH NO EXECUTION LAYER: the Board's view of it, at full weight, plus one sentence saying it is watched only and how enabling works. No illustration, no \"get started\". Frame C3.\n10 · A GLOBAL ACTIVITY FEED: no. Its three jobs already have homes — what needs you is the Inbox, what happened is the project's session list, what is true now is the Board. A global feed is precisely the attention machine §5 is trying to prevent, and it is the one thing on the reference app that would quietly rewrite this product's register."
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "w1l6",
      "x": 4620,
      "y": 2880,
      "name": "Where the design pushes back",
      "width": 2980,
      "height": 210,
      "content": "ONE DISAGREEMENT AND TWO EXTENSIONS THE BRIEF ASKED FOR.\nTHE COUNTER RULE (§5) IS RIGHT, WITH ONE AMENDMENT. \"Exactly one counter is legitimate\" holds. But column counts on the Kanban are not badges and are not activity — they are the number of cards in a column, verifiable by looking. They are set as plain monospace numerals in the fact register, beside the column name, with no pill and no colour. If that reads as a badge in build, delete them; nothing depends on them.\nRUNNING TREATMENT (§7.1): the honest answer really is a single quiet indicator and nothing else. Motion is not permissible here — not because animation is ugly, but because the one thing the design must achieve is that a running agent is IGNORABLE while a blocked agent is UNMISSABLE, and motion is the strongest attention signal available. Spending it on the thing you should ignore inverts the product.\nAGENT IDENTITY (§7.2): a fixed two-letter monogram in a hairline box, always at the same position on a card, always in $muted. Shape and position carry identity. No avatars (they imply other humans), no colour-per-agent (it would collide with the status palette and there are ten-plus agents), no icons (they imply capability). Ten agents shown in frame C5; the monograms stay distinguishable at 9px because they are read positionally, not semantically.\nWHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT: per-project Overview and per-project drift board (D22), triggers and cron automations, the YAML/CLI surface, project settings screens, onboarding, empty-state illustrations, the portfolio cost panel, and any \"+ New\" control in the portfolio layer."
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "w1l7",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 5280,
      "name": "Dark mode — designed, not inverted",
      "width": 2980,
      "height": 150,
      "content": "DARK IS THE SECOND-MONITOR MODE, and the ambient glance happens there more than anywhere. Three frames are drawn in it: the working shell, the goal rails at the cap, and the pre-flight — chosen because they are the frames whose meaning depends most on colour surviving the swap.\nWhat changes: the surface tokens, and the three status values to their verified dark pair (#0faaaa / #c3871e / #eb5f73 — the values that passed the dataviz validator; not substituted by eye).\nWhat does not change: the hierarchy, the monospace-means-machine-fact rule, the 3px running rail (it is $muted in both modes and reads as a rail, not a highlight), the monogram boxes, or the relative weight of the drift band against everything around it. The pre-flight in dark is the test case — $drift on a dark surface must stay a warning rather than becoming the brightest thing on screen, which is why the band is a hairline stroke and coloured text rather than a filled block."
    }
  ],
